.TH CLASSIFY 1 2026-10-25 "blocktools 0.1.0" "blocktools manual"
.SH NAME
classify \- unknown wallet material \-> public JSON lines, one per item:
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
classify [FILE] (or stdin) \-> {"class","line","fp"} per item; no secret is ever echoed
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
.RS 2
  {"tool":"classify","status":"ok","class":"mnemonic","line":1,"fp":"ab12..9f0e"}
.RE
.fi
Reads FILE (one argument) or stdin, at most 16 MiB. Never echoes secrets: secret items get a
SHA\-256 fingerprint (first/last 4 hex); only addresses ("address") and xpubs ("xpub") are echoed.
Classes: see blocktools_core::classify::CLASSES. Use it to pick the reader (recipes/recover.sh).
.SH OPTIONS
No flags. Input is read from stdin or the arguments shown in SYNOPSIS.
.SH INPUT/OUTPUT
classify: unknown wallet material \-> public JSON lines, one per item:
Reads FILE (one argument) or stdin, at most 16 MiB. Never echoes secrets: secret items get a
.SH EXIT STATUS
Measured on Linux by the EXAMPLES below (scripts/gen\-man.py runs them):
.TP
0
last status ok
.PP
Not every tool uses the same code for bad input: some exit 1, others exit 2 (docs/FINDINGS.md D1\-14). Always read the last JSON status line. Status and failure codes: docs/REFUSALS.md.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
$ echo "abandon abandon abandon abandon abandon abandon abandon abandon abandon abandon abandon about" | classify
# exit 0, last status: ok
.fi
.SH SECURITY NOTES
DANGEROUS: use at your own risk. Code that handles keys and signs transactions should always be read and audited by you before you trust it with real funds. Used incorrectly, it can destroy your money.
Used incorrectly (wrong address, huge fee, lost keys, wrong chain) you lose funds; verify every transaction.
It opens hostile wallet files and extracts secrets: run it only sealed and offline (./blocktools run).
Unit tests are evidence, not a substitute for your own review. See docs/SECURITY.md.
.SH SEE ALSO
blocktools(7), docs/SECURITY.md, docs/getting\-started.md, recipes/README.md
